issues.body:
## Current execution gate — 2026-09-19

**Dependency-ready for implementation from current main.**

- #27/MS-000 is landed and stable for this use.
- #28/MS-001 is merged through PR #44, with the bounded post-merge closure review/fixes merged through PR #54.
- The Materials Laboratory readiness prerequisite is satisfied by the landed schema-2 contract.
- Draft PR #42 is a historical pre-gate design/preflight record only. Do **not** merge or resume it as an implementation branch.
- Start INT-000 implementation from current main on a fresh isolated branch after reconciling #42's useful inventory/design findings against the landed source.
- First implementation work preserves current material behavior: stable schema/IDs, provenance-visible sparse/layered resolution and shadow/migration regression proof first. Broad material tuning remains later bounded versioned passes.

## Status and sequencing

**INT-000 — planned intermaterial-interaction infrastructure and campaign.
This is not a chemistry-only issue and not a blanket retune.**

This issue was originally named CHEM-000. The clarified scope is
**intermaterial interactions other than kinetic/material-motion physics**.

Execution-order rules:
- planning/inventory/schema work may begin early;
- implementation requires a stable #27 MicroScenario fixture contract plus the
  #28 **Materials Laboratory readiness checkpoint**;
- it does **not** require all of #28 to close or a blanket #14/G-final;
- every accepted tuning pass must pin the relevant current kinetic/contact
  baseline that affects how its materials meet;
- later relevant physics changes trigger targeted re-screening, not destruction
  of prior evidence.

Canonical ordering/scope:
[`docs/operations/microscenarios-programme.md`](https://github.com/techrote/cybersand/blob/main/docs/operations/microscenarios-programme.md).

## Problem

CyberSand already has dozens of materials and can grow toward 256 IDs. A naive
manual ordered-pair table grows quadratically: 64 materials imply 4096 ordered
pairs; 256 imply 65536. Real behavior also depends on state/context, so manually
authoring/tuning every matrix cell would be unmanageable and would make repeated
passes prone to destroying earlier improvements.

Most material combinations should inherit meaningful general behavior or be
explicitly non-interacting; only genuine exceptions need bespoke pair rules.

## Scope boundary

INT-000 owns non-kinetic material/material semantics such as:

- chemical reactions and explicit products/byproducts;
- combustion/ignition/extinguishing behavior;
- thermal-contact consequences and phase transitions;
- dissolution, corrosion and purification;
- curing/setting and environment-dependent conversion;
- electrical/conductive interaction;
- catalytic, growth/biological or other material-specific state interactions;
- wetting/absorption where it is an intermaterial state rule rather than merely
  motion;
- authoritative emissions/products caused by an interaction.

INT-000 does **not** own kinetic/material-motion physics such as:

- density exchange;
- pressure/flow and liquid movement;
- granular support, bridging or bracing;
- erosion/entrainment and physical mixing;
- collision, ballistic motion or Rapier coupling.

MicroScenarios may discover those defects. Capture/reduce them first, then route
them to the correct physics owner rather than encoding a compensating interaction
rule.

## Goal

Use MicroScenarios as the common interaction experiment substrate. One
interaction definition should be runnable:

- headlessly as characterization/regression;
- interactively in Materials Laboratory;
- in batch coverage campaigns;
- under human visual/feel review where useful.

Build permanent sparse/layered authoring, generated fixtures, versioned tuning
passes, provenance/coverage and comparison tooling so continued work remains
reviewable and reversible.

## Interaction definition model

Do not make the authored source of truth a dense N×N matrix.

Resolve intent through layers such as:

1. intrinsic material properties/state;
2. family/default interaction rules;
3. material-specific adjustments;
4. sparse directed/symmetric material-pair overrides;
5. context/state modifiers;
6. validated resolver/compiler into a compact runtime representation.

A dense resolved runtime lookup is allowed if profiling shows it is useful; dense
**manual authoring** is the thing to avoid.

Reuse/extend the #13 transport-profile precedent where appropriate: validated
profiles, effective values + provenance, family/material/pair layering, stable
IDs, explicit restart/owner boundaries and hashes. Do not force unrelated
interaction semantics into the transport profile merely for code reuse.

## Independent interaction channels

Avoid one monolithic "A versus B" record. Keep independently tunable channels so
changing one mechanism does not silently alter another, for example:

- thermal/phase;
- dissolution/corrosion;
- chemical conversion/products;
- combustion;
- electrical;
- curing/growth/special state;
- authoritative emissions/byproducts.

Kinetic/contact settings remain under existing owners and may be displayed
read-only in a workbench for diagnostic provenance.

## Versioned tuning passes

Do not overwrite history with one mutable "current matrix".

Each accepted pass must:

- preserve a named baseline;
- record exact rules/families/pairs/parameters changed;
- pin scenario/schema/source/runtime/material/profile identities;
- compare against the previous accepted pass;
- rerun all affected previously accepted regressions;
- retain rejected/superseded candidates and old evidence;
- state accept / accept-with-limitation / reject / defer.

Passes should normally be bounded by mechanism/family, e.g. aqueous/dissolution,
thermal/phase, combustion, corrosive/reactive, electrical/special and sparse
explicit exceptions—not "material 1 versus every other ID".

## Rolling kinetic/contact baselines

There is no permanent global "physics-contact-baseline-v1" prerequisite.

Every accepted interaction result records the exact source revision and relevant
Water/granular/transport/contact policies that determined whether/how materials
met. If later kinetic work changes that contact regime, flag only affected
interaction evidence for re-screening.

This keeps separate questions separate:
- reaction kinetics/cadence wrong;
- materials meet too rarely/often because transport changed;
- product created with wrong support/state;
- presentation changed while conversion did not.

## Generated fixture families

For a candidate family/pair/channel, generate only relevant topologies, e.g.:

- A above B / B above A;
- horizontal interface;
- small quantity/droplet into bulk;
- sustained stream/contact;
- mixed/packed contact;
- heated/cooled starts when temperature matters;
- ignited contact for combustion;
- separated-then-release;
- barrier/control material comparison;
- low/high quantity and short/long contact horizons.

Not every topology applies to every pair. The generator should state why each
fixture exists.

## Required observations

Where applicable record:

- exact starting/ending material/species quantities;
- explicit conversions/products/byproducts;
- source/sink/outflow ledgers;
- Water/fractional quantity accounting under its contract;
- temperature/phase/lifetime/state changes;
- reaction/contact attempts and successes where instrumented;
- gas/smoke/fire/product creation;
- time to first reaction and quiescence/steady state;
- activity/wake/work/performance;
- repeat/worker parity;
- scenario/profile/source/runtime identity.

A pleasing visual result never waives conservation/ownership/capacity contracts.

## Interaction Workbench / Materials Laboratory integration

Materials Laboratory should load any generated fixture, reproduce the same
declared geometry/seed/profile and expose its observation record.

The workbench may show an **effective** matrix/coverage view, but the matrix is
not the authored source of truth. A cell should be able to show:

- resolved values per interaction channel;
- rule origin/provenance;
- directed/symmetric status;
- explicit overrides and conflicts;
- relevant tests/evidence;
- what changed in the selected tuning pass.

Human tuning workflow:

1. choose mechanism/family/pair;
2. choose generated topology/preset;
3. run previous accepted baseline;
4. adjust only admitted parameters;
5. A/B against the baseline;
6. record observation;
7. accept/reject/defer;
8. retain both old and new evidence.

## Coverage model

Maintain a ledger distinguishing at least:

- inherited/default behavior;
- explicitly non-interacting;
- characterized and accepted;
- accepted with known limitation;
- rejected candidate;
- superseded tuning choice with retained evidence;
- explicit pair/material override;
- untested/not yet characterized.

Coverage percentage must never imply that all N² ordered pairs deserve bespoke
rules.

## Organic discovery integration

Generated pair fixtures are not the only way to find work. Exploratory #28 and
later worlds should feed INT-000:

play world -> capture anomaly -> reduce fixture -> triage subsystem -> bounded
fix/tuning -> retain regression -> return to world.

An anomaly discovered in an interaction-rich world may ultimately belong to a
kinetic owner. Do not force it into INT-000 merely because two materials were
present.

## Initial interaction campaign seeds

Start apparatus validation from existing observed mechanisms such as:

- Water / Salt / Brine;
- Water / Lava / Ice / Steam;
- Acid / Metal / Rust;
- Fire / Wood / Oil / Coal / Dust;
- Sodium / Water;
- Cement / Concrete environment-dependent cure;
- Toxic Sludge purification;
- Spark / conductive Metal behavior.

These seed fixture/tool validation. They do not authorize broad tuning in the
infrastructure issue.

## Non-goals

- No immediate retune of every interaction.
- No assumption every ordered pair is meaningful.
- No broad material-registry redesign unless separately admitted.
- No relaxation of ownership, bounded writes, determinism, failure quarantine or
  exact ledgers.
- No hidden coupling of kinetic tuning and interaction tuning.
- No scenario-specific interaction scripts replacing authoritative material rules.

## Acceptance criteria

- A versioned sparse layered interaction/coverage representation exists.
- Independent channels prevent unrelated tuning from being overwritten together.
- Generated fixtures run both headlessly and in Materials Laboratory from the
  same definition.
- Directed/symmetric inheritance and sparse overrides are explicit/testable.
- Effective values can report provenance and selected-pass changes.
- Baseline + subsequent pass comparison preserves old evidence and identifies
  every changed interaction/parameter.
- Representative existing interaction families have generated fixtures and
  correct accounting without changing production semantics merely to prove the
  apparatus.
- Batch output reports coverage gaps rather than fabricating results for
  untested pairs.
- Determinism/worker/conservation/source-sink checks are included where supported.
- Rolling kinetic/contact baseline identity and targeted revalidation are
  represented explicitly.
- Follow-on tuning can be split into bounded versioned family/mechanism issues
  with regression protection.
- Canonical RAG programme/roadmap/retrieval routes remain synchronized.

## Completion result

INT-000 establishes the intermaterial experimentation/authoring/evidence system.
Actual material retuning proceeds in later bounded passes, using these fixtures
so improvements remain reviewable, reversible and protected from later passes.

## Purpose

Bounded corrective follow-up to completed #26 / merged PR #41.

**This is an apparatus/evidence repair, not a reopening of #26 Water semantics and not #45 implementation.**

The post-merge independent review of #26 found that the two rejected local candidates remain legitimately rejected, but the retained `water_issue26` apparatus has acceptance-system defects that must be corrected before #45 may use it to admit, accept or reject a new saturated-head mechanism.

## Governing disposition

Preserve all #26 historical archives, hashes, candidate commits, negative results and Outcome-B decision.

Do **not**:
- rewrite historical #26 outputs as though they were produced by corrected metrics;
- resurrect the rejected head-scaled or radius-2-horizon candidates;
- combine those rejected candidates;
- change production Water semantics;
- start #45 head/pressure-transmission implementation in this issue;
- start #18 compact directional history.

The repair creates a **new versioned apparatus/baseline** for future successor experiments.

## Current downstream routing

#49 remains the sole owner of the #26 successor-apparatus correction and may proceed independently of the WEX research lanes. Current downstream routing is **#49 corrected apparatus + available WEX evidence -> #95 synthesis -> #45 proceed/narrow/recharter/defer/no-go -> directional-reference reassessment -> #18**. #91 is now landed characterization evidence to consume as context, not scope to absorb. #95 owns the downstream synthesis/disposition; #49 must not absorb WEX sheet/gas/decompression work or REM implementation work. This routing clarification does **not** change #49's implementation scope, repair contract or acceptance criteria.

## Review findings that must be repaired

### 1. False-pass equilibrium/settling metric

Current `surface_stats()` ignores dry columns. A mass-conserving world can therefore concentrate Water in a subset of columns while reporting an extremely small surface spread. An entirely empty measurement region also collapses to a nominal zero spread.

The current “sustained” threshold latches after five passing 60-tick samples and is not revoked by later failure. Unreached thresholds use numeric `0`, which can contaminate ordinary medians as if it meant instant success.

Required repair:
- define fixture-specific expected wet coverage and/or mass/geometry-derived equilibrium;
- represent `not_reached` explicitly, never as success-valued zero;
- define sustained acceptance as a preregistered passing suffix/window that cannot remain accepted after subsequent failure unless the contract explicitly allows that;
- add adversarial unit tests for dry-column concentration, empty ROI, transient-pass-then-regress, and mixed reached/censored reductions.

### 2. U-tube topology is not the clean two-limb control claimed

The retained `unequal-head-u-tube` leaves the centre open above the lower connecting passage, creating a third open compartment. Preserve this historical fixture/result, but qualify it as an unequal-head communicating-reservoir fixture rather than a clean two-limb U-tube.

Required repair:
- version the historical fixture without changing its old geometry/reference;
- add a new clean two-limb unequal-head fixture with an explicitly roofed connecting passage;
- define the mass-derived/common-level equilibrium target and ROIs for all communicating compartments;
- generate a new source-matched control baseline for the new fixture before #45 candidate code.

### 3. Performance interpretation

The prior report compared whole-run p95 across runs with different active/sleep proportions. The cost increase is real, but the dramatic p95 ratio must not be described as persistent active-tick cost without a dedicated trace.

Required repair:
- separate startup/active-tick cost, time/work to quiescence, sleeping cost, and total run cost;
- retain existing historical timing values with a qualification rather than rewriting them;
- update #45 instructions to use the separated timing contract.

### 4. Surface-defect proxies are too coarse for successor acceptance

Current terrace/hill/contact-gap proxies are useful diagnostics but do not fully implement the declared #26 authoritative/rendered lifetime semantics. Sampling every 60 ticks cannot establish high-frequency shimmer.

Required repair:
- label historical surface metrics as sampled proxies;
- add fixture-appropriate contour/coverage retention sufficient to measure the intended authoritative quantities;
- distinguish a monotonic slope from discrete terrace shelves;
- make wall-contact expectations geometry/time aware;
- if rendered acceptance is required later, define it separately from authority and do not infer it from native-only metrics.

### 5. Evidence runner hardening

`tools/physics/water_issue26.py` can overwrite an existing output directory, relies on Python `assert` for evidence-critical validation, and does not robustly retain every timeout/nonzero/malformed attempt before aborting.

Required repair:
- refuse a non-empty destination by default;
- use explicit validation exceptions/checks;
- retain structured records for timeout, nonzero exit and malformed output;
- emit a completion manifest only when every registered case has an accounted disposition;
- add reducer/runner tests for corruption, censored values and overwrite refusal.

### 6. Documentation/provenance corrections

Record explicitly:
- final PR #41 validation provided a Windows **cross-build** plus Linux runtime regression; it was not fresh Windows runtime execution;
- draft PR #33 remains historical H evidence and did not merge; explain exactly which evidence was consumed/reconciled by #26 rather than leaving the old registration condition ambiguous;
- closed #26 remains Outcome B, but its apparatus v1 cannot govern #45 acceptance without this repair.

## Required source review

Before editing:
- read `AGENTS.md`;
- read #26, #45, #18, #90, #91, #95, #79 and #87, plus the current architecture/MicroScenarios ordering;
- read the current REM RAG at `docs/operations/development-claims-remediation-programme.md`;
- read:
  - `docs/audits/2026-09-19-issue-26-registration.md`
  - `docs/audits/2026-09-19-issue-26-water-leveling.md`
  - `docs/systems/water-design.md`
  - `docs/operations/architecture-programme.md`
  - `docs/reference/status-and-roadmap.md`
  - `docs/reference/validation-evidence.md`
  - `native/bench/water_issue26.cpp`
  - `tools/physics/water_issue26.py`

Inspect actual current `origin/main`, working tree, concurrent #12/#28/#29 work and required tool/dependency state. Use an isolated worktree/branch and preserve unrelated work.

## Implementation / evidence sequence

1. Freeze a **correction registration** before changing metric/fixture behavior.
2. Add adversarial tests that reproduce every confirmed apparatus defect.
3. Correct/version metrics and evidence runner without changing production Water.
4. Preserve old fixture IDs/results; introduce new fixture/metric schema versions.
5. Run corrected current-Water baselines needed for successor use.
6. Demonstrate that the adversarial false-pass cases now fail or are explicitly censored.
7. Run worker-parity/mass/boundedness checks and relevant native/docs/retrieval validation.
8. Update canonical RAG/status/handover records and #45/#18 instructions.
9. Open a PR; merge only after required automated checks pass.
10. Verify merge on `main` before closing this issue.

## Acceptance

Complete only when:

- all confirmed false-pass/censoring cases have regression tests;
- old #26 evidence remains immutable and explicitly qualified;
- a versioned corrected two-limb communicating-head fixture exists with a fresh control baseline;
- successor equilibrium metrics cannot pass merely because expected columns are dry;
- `not_reached` cannot reduce as an instant success;
- sustained criteria behave according to the frozen contract after later regression;
- performance reporting separates active/startup/sleep/total work;
- runner/evidence output is overwrite-safe and failure-retaining;
- platform and PR #33 provenance wording is accurate;
- #45 cannot proceed to implementation/acceptance using apparatus v1;
- no production Water semantic change is introduced.

## Completion and downstream routing — 2026-09-20

#49 is complete through PR #104 / authoritative-main merge `4e4381d304458ef9b0dfa9336cfe08e8cc162210`.

Final PR head: `ef98212079fe2cabefcc77e09183c0cbea624052`.

Post-merge validation on the landed main commit:
- Documentation and provenance `35538230433` — success;
- Issue 49 Water apparatus v2 `35538230436` — success;
- Native C++ validation `35538230422` — success;
- GDExtension build and current Godot regression `35538230427` — success.

Canonical correction/evidence:
- `docs/audits/2026-09-20-issue-49-water-apparatus-v2-registration.md`;
- `docs/audits/2026-09-20-issue-49-water-apparatus-v2-baseline.md`.

The apparatus-side prerequisite is now satisfied. This does **not** independently authorize #45 implementation or numeric acceptance. Current routing remains:

**#49 corrected apparatus + available WEX evidence → #95 synthesis → #45 proceed/narrow/recharter/defer/no-go → directional-reference reassessment → #18**

#26 remains closed as valid negative evidence for its exact tested candidates. Apparatus v1 remains historical/qualified evidence. Production Water semantics are unchanged. #18 remains held pending the downstream evidence-based disposition.

